# Copy this file to .env and edit as needed:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# .env is gitignored. mise loads it into the environment of every task, so
# `mise run run/api` picks these up automatically. A missing .env is fine —
# every setting below has a default baked into the binary.
#
# ── HOW THIS RELATES TO THE FLAGS ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every command-line flag has an environment variable behind it, named by
# upper-casing the flag and replacing dashes with underscores. Precedence is:
#
#     command-line flag  >  environment variable  >  built-in default
#
# so you can still override any single setting for one run without editing this
# file: `go run ./cmd/api -port=4001`.
#
# `go run ./cmd/api -help` lists every setting with its default AND its
# environment variable, and is generated from the flags themselves — so it can't
# fall out of date with this file. Treat it as the reference; this file is only
# the subset worth setting locally.
#
# The same variables are what you put in a container's `env_file:` or a
# Kubernetes Secret. Nothing here is specific to mise or to local development.

# ── Server ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# GREENLIGHT_PORT=4000
# GREENLIGHT_ENV=development

# ── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Path to the SQLite file. Used by the API, the seeder, and the db/* mise tasks
# alike, so setting it here points all of them at the same database.
# GREENLIGHT_DB_DSN=greenlight.db

# ── Rate limiter ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# GREENLIGHT_LIMITER_RPS=2
# GREENLIGHT_LIMITER_BURST=4
# GREENLIGHT_LIMITER_ENABLED=true

# ── SMTP ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Leave GREENLIGHT_SMTP_HOST unset to have emails written to the application log
# instead of sent — that's the default, and it's enough to register a user and
# read the activation token straight out of the logs with no mail account at all.
#
# The password is the reason this file exists. Passing it as -smtp-password
# would put the credential in `ps aux`, where every user on the host can read it.

# GREENLIGHT_SMTP_HOST=sandbox.smtp.mailtrap.io
# GREENLIGHT_SMTP_PORT=25
# GREENLIGHT_SMTP_USERNAME=
# GREENLIGHT_SMTP_PASSWORD=
# GREENLIGHT_SMTP_SENDER=Greenlight <no-reply@greenlight.example.com>

# ── CORS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Space separated, exactly as the flag takes it.

# GREENLIGHT_CORS_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=https://app.example.com https://admin.example.com
